/*
PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Given a string s, return the longest palindromic substring in s.

LEETCODE QUESTION NO: 5

APPROACH:
1. Consider every character as the center of an odd-length palindrome.
2. Consider every gap between two characters as the center of an even-length palindrome.
3. Expand left and right while characters are equal.
4. Whenever a longer palindrome is found, update its starting index and length.
5. Return the longest palindrome using substr().

TIME COMPLEXITY: O(n^2)
SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(1)
*/

class Solution {
public:
    string longestPalindrome(string s) {
        int n = s.size();
        int start = 0;
        int maxLen = 1;

        auto expand = [&](int left, int right) {
            while(left >= 0 && right < n && s[left] == s[right]) {
                if(right - left + 1 > maxLen) {
                    maxLen = right - left + 1;
                    start = left;
                }
                left--;
                right++;
            }
        };

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            expand(i, i);       // Odd length
            expand(i, i + 1);   // Even length
        }

        return s.substr(start, maxLen);
    }
};

/*
MISTAKES TO AVOID:
1. Checking only odd-length palindromes.
2. Forgetting the even-length case: expand(i, i + 1).
3. Updating start before checking whether the palindrome is actually longer.
4. Using wrong length formula:
   length = right - left + 1

LEARNING:
- Every palindrome can be identified by its center.
- Odd palindrome -> one center character.
- Even palindrome -> center lies between two characters.
- Expand from the center instead of generating every substring.

OPTIMIZATION:
Brute force checking every substring and then checking whether it is a palindrome
can take O(n^3). Expanding around every center reduces it to O(n^2).
*/
